            <ArticleTitle>Specialized Weaponry: How a Type III-A CRISPR-Cas System Excels at Combating Phages.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>258-259</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1931-3128(17)30352-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.chom.2017.08.019</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>CRISPR-Cas-mediated defense against phage invaders usually requires recognition of short sequences, termed protospacer-adjacent motifs (PAMs), in phage DNA. In this issue of Cell Host &amp; Microbe, Pyenson et al. (2017) show that the lack of a PAM requirement in some CRISPR-Cas systems prevents interference evasion and facilitates phage extinction.</AbstractText>
